EXEC(2)                        P32W Unix                         EXEC(2)

NAME
       exec, execve, execv, execl, execvp, execlp - execute a file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       int execve(const char *path, char *const argv[],
                  char *const envp[]);
       int execv(const char *path, char *const argv[]);
       int execl(const char *path, const char *arg0, ...);
       int execvp(const char *file, char *const argv[]);
       int execlp(const char *file, const char *arg0, ...);

       extern char **environ;

DESCRIPTION
       execve replaces the program of the calling process with the
       program in the file path. Only execve is a system call; the
       others are library routines that call it.

       The file must be a regular file with execute permission for
       the caller (for the super-user, at least one x bit) and a
       P32W a.out (see a.out(5)): magic number 0x50333277 and an
       entry point inside the text. The new address space has the
       text at 0x1000 (read and execute only), the data and bss
       from the next page boundary (read and write), the break just
       after the bss, and the stack below 0x800000. The kernel
       copies the argument and environment strings onto the new
       stack and starts the program at its entry point with the
       stack pointer at argc, followed by argv[0] ... argv[argc-1],
       a null pointer, the environment pointers and a null
       pointer. The C start-up (crt0) calls main(argc, argv, envp)
       and sets environ.

       argv and envp are arrays of string pointers ending with a
       null pointer; either may itself be a null pointer, meaning
       an empty list. By convention argv[0] is the program's name.

       If the file has the set-user-ID bit (04000), the effective
       user ID becomes the file's owner; with the set-group-ID bit
       (02000), the effective group ID becomes the file's group.
       The real IDs do not change.

       Open files stay open (there is no close-on-exec). Signals
       that were caught revert to the default action; ignored
       signals stay ignored. The current directory, umask, real
       IDs, controlling terminal, pending alarm, CPU times and
       process ID are kept. The process's name, as ps(1) shows it,
       becomes the last component of path (15 characters at most).
       A traced process (ptrace(2)) stops with SIGTRAP before the
       first instruction of the new program.

       execv(path, argv) is execve(path, argv, environ).

       execl(path, arg0, arg1, ..., (char *)0) collects its
       arguments into an argv array and calls execv.

       execvp and execlp are execv and execl, but when file
       contains no / they look for it in each directory of the
       PATH environment variable in turn (a colon-separated list;
       an empty entry is the current directory; without PATH,
       /bin:/usr/bin). They go on to the next directory when an
       attempt fails with ENOENT or EACCES and stop at any other
       error.

RETURN VALUE
       On success the call does not return: the new program runs.
       On failure the old program continues, the call returns -1
       and errno says why.

ERRORS
       ENOENT  The file, or a directory in path, does not exist.
               (execvp, execlp: not found in any PATH directory.)
       ENOTDIR A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in the
               path; or the file is not a regular file; or it has
               no execute permission.
       ENOEXEC The file is not a P32W a.out (wrong magic number,
               an entry point outside the text, or it is too
               short).
       E2BIG   More than 63 arguments or 63 environment strings,
               or more than 4096 bytes of strings in all.
       ENOMEM  Not enough memory for the new image.
       EFAULT  path, argv, envp or one of their strings is a bad
               address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       fork(2), exit(2), ptrace(2), a.out(5), sh(1)

BUGS
       The kernel knows nothing of #! interpreter files; execvp
       and execlp do not run a file that fails with ENOEXEC as a
       shell script, as V7's did. There is no execle; execvp does
       not report EACCES when it saw one, only ENOENT. execl and
       execlp silently drop all arguments after the 32nd.
